    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_1J8QK4XTB6PM09VZDR3HNW5CEY",
      id: "v2-emitter-native-crate-partition",
      owner: "self-host",
      carriers: carrier(path: "src/v2/workflow/rust_crate_partition.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: updated(t: fields(
        headline: "Decide how the self-built compiler is split into separately built pieces",
        boundary: "The compiler the new generator emits is today written out as ONE package: one manifest, one crate root, and roughly a hundred and seventy generated files beneath it. This step derives the split from the compiler's own module graph instead — group the modules that genuinely depend on each other in a cycle, keep the groups acyclic, give every module exactly one owning piece, and generate a real multi-package workspace from that. The seven-piece split that already exists is for the OLD compiler and is assigned by hand; it is a precedent for the machinery, not a layout to copy.",
        displaced_cost: "Where the boundaries fall decides which declarations have to be visible outside their own file, whether the pieces' interfaces are actually complete, what a one-line edit rebuilds, and how much memory a build needs. A one-package artifact answers none of those questions, so proving the compiler can rebuild itself over it proves it for a shape nobody intends to keep — and the failures it would hide are exactly the ones that surface first when the shape changes.",
        first_slice: "Read the real closure into the module-graph carrier that already exists — modules, dependency edges, exported declarations, by-value type rows — and condense the mutually-dependent groups. Selecting the split comes after there is a graph to select over; a target count alone is not a policy, and the resolver that takes one is deliberately unimplemented today.",
        red_control: "A module owned by two pieces, or by none, refuses. A cycle between pieces refuses. A declaration one piece needs and another does not expose must fail the build rather than be papered over by exporting everything. And a one-line edit to a leaf must be shown to rebuild that leaf's owner and the pieces that depend on it — NOT the whole compiler, which is what a single package always does and is why crate count alone is not the claim.",
        out_of_scope: "Not inventing piece names up front, not copying the old compiler's hand-made layout, and not blocking the first real module flip or the initial native CI routing — those establish different facts and can run on the one-package artifact meanwhile, provided that artifact is carried as a temporary probe with this step as its end condition.",
        handback: "The derived module graph, the split and what selected it, the generated workspace, a build and behavioral run of the same corpus through it, a deliberately broken cross-piece export that fails, and a receipt showing a leaf edit rebuilding only its own dependents."
      ), on: "2026-09-30", note: "Awaiting the operator's acceptance event: src/v2/test/claim/stage0_partition_rebuild_scope_witness_test.dag over gunbc.stage0_partition_rebuild_scope; evidence Pkg7a/b #11961, Pkg7d/e #12214.")),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "v2-emitter-production", contribution: "The emitted closure split into crates is what makes each generation buildable."),
      centering: plan(text: "Run the named test process on src/v2/test/claim:stage0_partition_rebuild_scope_witness_test (covers gunbc.stage0_partition_rebuild_scope). Confirm green at head, and that the partition from Pkg7a/b (#11961) and Pkg7d/e (#12214) is the one gunbc.stage0_partition_package_graph builds. Change nothing. Hand back the receipt. Stop if red."),
    ),
