// CONSTRUCTION RESIDUE declared by merged #6777 (emit: deny unreachable_patterns). Authority:
// src/v1/stage0_crates.dag unreachable_patterns_deny_note — validation landed; construction fix
// (group arms sharing an outer constructor into a nested match in freemonoid_match_arm_for) remains
// follow-on emitter work per that note, not landed on main. Pre-existing; NOT namespace scope; runs
// independently of N3-B waves.
//
// REPRODUCER (live on main): v1.gunbc.occurrence_binding_parser_walk
// declaration_exposure_from_containment — containment.ancestors match with depth-two Cons nesting:
// Empty => ModuleExposure; Cons { tail: Empty } => ModuleExposure; Cons { tail: Cons { head: _,
// tail: _ } } => LexicalExposure via occurrence_containment_parent_scope or ModuleExposure
// fallback.
//
// LOCALIZATION: v1_gunbc_occurrence_binding_parser_walk occurrence_containment_parent_scope in the
// same generated file lowers correctly (empty ancestors -> None, else -> Some(...)), so this is not
// a blanket FreeMonoid lowering failure: single-arm-per-outer-constructor lowering is correct in
// the one observed case. The defect is specific to multiple match arms sharing an outer
// constructor: freemonoid_match_arm_for selects arms |> filter(variant match) |> first — a second
// arm sharing the outer variant label but carrying a different discriminator body is silently
// dropped.
//
// B0 WORKAROUND (substrate not on main): N3-B0 exposure-authority adds
// std.occurrence_binding_candidates declaration_exposure_namespace_structural_root — an emit-safe
// depth-one parent-scope rewrite replacing a depth-two nested FreeMonoid match the emitter would
// collapse; deliberate scaffold, not a fix.
//
// DISSOLUTION TRIGGER — That B0 rewrite exists only because freemonoid_match_arm_for drops sibling
// arms sharing an outer constructor. Once the follow-on construction fix lands and generated output
// re-verifies, the rewrite may return to the direct nested match. Re-verify against generated
// output, not source, before dissolving.
//
// GENERATED DISCREPANCY (live on main): v1_gunbc_occurrence_binding_parser_walk
// declaration_exposure_from_containment — both the __fm.is_empty() branch and the else branch emit
// identical ModuleExposure { module }, where the source distinguishes empty-ancestor ModuleExposure
// from nested ModuleExposure/LexicalExposure. Expected after construction fix: three distinct
// outcomes (empty ancestors -> ModuleExposure; single ancestor -> ModuleExposure; nested ancestors
// -> LexicalExposure via occurrence_containment_parent_scope, ModuleExposure fallback when parent
// scope is absent).

// N3-C (namespace-pool-independence) LANE BRIEF — operator ruling 2026-08-07. Undispatched until B1
// lands; starts from the cleaned final B1 head, not from B0 and not from an intermediate B1 draft.
//
// CONTRACT: Compare two complete B1 clause-(e) projection results for the same source subject —
// baseline projection versus perturbed projection with one unrelated loaded file added. Required
// equality across both: same source subject; same reference occurrence identities; same selected
// declaration identities; same bound-reference population; same occurrence-level provenance rows;
// same file dependencies; same exact refusal outcome when applicable.
//
// LOAD-BEARING RED CONTROL: A perturbation that reselects a different declaration inside the same
// provider file while leaving the file-dependency set unchanged must be REFUSED by the full-carrier
// comparison. A dependency-set-only comparison passes this case while missing the exact Class B
// reselection N3-C exists to forbid — the comparison is over the complete
// ReferenceDerivedDependencyProjection carrier, not the deduplicated file list alone.
//
// DEPENDENCIES: Requires B1 (ReferenceDerivedDependencyProjection plus occurrence-grain
// provenance). Does not require B2 (ordinary loaded compilation closure producer).

// FIVE WAYS THE BUILD REPORTED A RESULT NOBODY EARNED, mapped to their exact homes rather than
// gathered under an umbrella node (consolidation ruling, 2026-08-08 — the parallel authority branch
// proposed one observation-verification-integrity node covering all five; a coarse identity over
// mechanisms this graph already decomposes would be the §3 second authority, so the incident record
// ports and the node does not). The mapping, one row each: a remote build that exits zero while its
// output never comes back is compute-artifact-return-and-materialization's first refusal; a stale
// local binary compared against itself is the same row's manifest-content refusal plus
// ci-remote-build-containment's stale-artefact refusal; a re-run that replays a combination
// computed when the change was opened is ci-owned-execution's fresh-combination control; no checks
// at all treated as satisfied, and a cancelled job rendered as a generic failure, are both
// ci-check-projection's typed endings — unobserved and cancelled each a distinct answer, never
// absorbed into pass or fail. Every future incident of this family files against exactly one of
// those homes; a sixth mechanism with no home is a finding about the graph, not a reason to mint
// the umbrella.

// THE SIX CAPABILITIES INSIDE namespace-reference-derived-closure ARE NOT SIX NODES, and this cut
// adds exactly three rows in front of it rather than decomposing it (2026-08-04). Its closing
// contract, namespace_reference_derived_closure_contract, is a set-difference frontier over six
// capabilities under three triggers, and every row read Unavailable while the node itself was the
// only dispatchable thing in the lane. That is a single startable row standing for three separable
// pieces of work with different substrates, which is the coarse-node hazard
// namespace_node_recut_note already priced once.
//
// THE CUT FOLLOWS THE TRIGGERS, because the triggers already record which work each capability
// waits on. namespace-structural-observations owns the four capabilities under
// P2aStructuralCandidateProducer7515 — same-parse-tree rules, whose substrate is the merged
// occurrence-binding parser walk. namespace-cross-file-provenance owns the one under
// P2aReferenceDependencyProjection7515. namespace-pool-independence owns the one under
// P2aPoolIndependentDependencyProjection7515.
//
// THE FIRST TWO ARE PARALLEL, NOT SEQUENTIAL. No dependency runs from the same-file rules to the
// cross-file projection; they share only the contract they feed. Sequencing them would have idled
// one worker for no modeled reason. Pool independence DOES depend on cross-file provenance, because
// a differential needs a projector to perturb.
//
// WHY THE CROSS-FILE ROW IS THE LARGE ONE. The merged parser walk resolves nothing across a file
// boundary — its own authority note scopes it to authored source through parse_with_table, with
// transport and module path from the parser. So cross-file target realization sits INSIDE that row
// rather than upstream of it, and the trigger being named after a merged PR does not mean the
// capability is discharged.
//
// NO NEW IDENTITY WAS MINTED FOR THE INTEGRATION OR FOR THE CENSUS.
// namespace-reference-derived-closure keeps its durable identity and now denotes the aggregate
// handback — all six observations admitted, the expecting-red enrolment retired, its existing check
// green — so its first_slice moved to say so while its boundary, which still covers all six, did
// not. namespace-ambiguity-discharge likewise keeps its identity and its existing dependency on the
// closure node; the census it owns is authoritative only once all six close, never after the
// same-file rows alone, because a census taken before cross-file answers exist undercounts exactly
// the candidates that cross a file boundary and inherits the pool coincidence pool-independence
// exists to remove.
//
// THE THREE NEW ROWS ARE DELIBERATELY UNBOUND. Each first_slice names authoring its own closing
// check as its first act, per v1_lane_binding_survey_note: the contract follows the witness, never
// precedes it. Authoring contracts here to make the rows look startable is the coverage-by-illusion
// tier that note forbids, and pool-independence could not honestly carry one at all — its
// differential compares an output shape its own prerequisite has not yet produced.

// The operator explicitly accepted the three independent R0 models and the separate P−1 evidence
// carrier, then accepted R1 after merging its three-realization compatibility extraction, and
// accepted P0 after its layered landing spine and twenty-two executing journey witnesses landed. R1
// depends only on the three R0 receipts; P0 consumes both accepted R1 and accepted P−1. These
// receipts preserve every prerequisite and handback as durable facts. Replacing accepted rows with
// their child would erase history and violate roadmap_acceptance_receipt_note.

// P0 acceptance maps the roadmap clauses through
// test.claim.source_integration_landing_spine_witness (P0 journey witnesses; former roll-up deleted):
// all seven removed closure receipts stay machine-owned; evidence
// support/challenge/neither/conflict and terminal blocking do not become a preference question;
// both material alternatives resume by structural position; authorization, self-amendment,
// exhausted-bound, and permanent-effect failures keep their typed internal refusal and plain
// terminal projection; stale-parent work retries internally; a committed independently read-back
// transition stays Landed through projection failure; the audience projection excludes internal
// theory and manual Git-repair vocabulary; and the successful path retains its ordinary Git
// compatibility receipt. The same aggregate executes the explicit 50-agent/10-overlap/30-minute
// stress profile and timestamp non-authority. Handback remains bounded exactly at P0:
// gunbc.source_integration_landing_spine names the absent P1 proof kernel, P2 capture/edit lens,
// and P3 effect transport; none is claimed or activated by this receipt.

// ACCEPTED AT THE NARROWED BAR, WITH ONE HANDBACK CLAUSE GENUINELY AMENDED RATHER THAN REWORDED --
// read the digest move as a real change to the bar, which is exactly the check
// criteria_digest_reword_note asks a reviewer to perform. The clause as #7558 landed it demanded
// 'witnesses asserting exact values rather than lower bounds'. Those witnesses no longer exist on
// main: #7615 deleted the five that pinned live-population counts (7 dispatch sites, 183 derived
// rows, 2 declared rows, 174 arm identities, 161 authored spellings) because DESIGN.md 5 rules that
// a measurement copied from the same current tree is not an oracle -- and those five had already
// behaved exactly as that rule predicts, as a change detector that redded main twice (#7575 landed
// one derived arm short, #7614 repaired, #7615 removed the class). Accepting against the original
// wording would have required either restoring a forbidden oracle or calling a clause satisfied
// that plainly was not.
//
// WHAT REPLACED IT IS STRONGER, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS NOT A LOWERED BAR. The deleted clause existed
// so that a silently dropped arm stays detectable, which a lower-bound witness cannot do. Every
// exact assertion grounded in a NAMED identity or a controlled fixture survived #7615 untouched:
// free_call_shadowing_is_exactly_the_two_inert_lens_bridges and
// method_call_shadowing_is_exactly_the_known_lookup_case assert exact shadow sets by member name
// (CITATION CORRECTION, 2026-08-11: the first of those no longer exists under that name. The
// inert-lens census, its two host builtins and the v2.lens.inert_lens module were deleted whole, so
// the free-call shadow set emptied and the witness became free_call_carries_no_shadowed_spelling,
// an exact set assertion over an empty set rather than over two named members. That is a genuinely
// weaker witness than the one this note cites as surviving evidence -- an empty-set claim cannot
// discriminate a detector that never reports -- and the discrimination is carried instead by the
// planted-duplicate controls and by method_call_shadowing_is_exactly_the_known_lookup_case, which
// still pins a named member. Recorded here rather than silently reworded, because the sentence was
// cited as evidence and the evidence changed);
// the_two_shadowed_arm_identities_under_lookup_are_exactly_the_expected_pair pins both identities;
// the_declared_residue_is_exactly_the_two_non_match_arm_sites is a two-directional set equality by
// name rather than a count. Only the tree-copied census literals went, which is precisely the line
// DESIGN.md 5 draws when it says the rule rejects tree-copied census pins while preserving
// exact-count fixture tests. The three denominators were NOT deleted: dispatch_site_count,
// distinct_arm_identity_count and authored_spelling_count all remain derived from the roster rather
// than pinned as literals, which is what this node's handback asks for. AN EARLIER DRAFT OF THIS
// NOTE OVERCLAIMED WHAT CONSUMES THEM, and the correction is recorded here rather than quietly
// edited because it is the same failure this repository keeps paying for -- a sentence that reads
// well while the evidence it cites does not establish it (caught in review 46995). That draft said
// all three 'gained a live consumer at D1' which 'joins census to roster by identity'. Neither half
// was true. What test.claim.primitive_identity_join_witness
// w_interpreter_census_consumes_roster_authority actually executes is distinct_arm_identity_count,
// authored_spelling_count and v1_interpreter_row_count: it asserts the ordering relation the
// deleted witness used to pin, plus primitive_d0_interpreter_census_matches_roster, which is
// primitive_d0_interpreter_surface_row_count() == v1_interpreter_row_count() -- a COUNT EQUALITY
// between two independent derivations of the same population, not an identity join. That is a
// legitimate cross-derivation reconciliation and it is not a tree-copied census literal, but
// DESIGN.md 5 is explicit that completeness is an identity join rather than a count equality, so it
// must not be described as one. And dispatch_site_count has NO external consumer at all: the only
// mention outside its own carrier is this note. That is named here as residue rather than counted
// as delivered. DISSOLVE-ON: the D0 identity join reaching arm grain, at which point the census and
// the roster reconcile by row identity and the count equality is replaced rather than supplemented.
//
// EVIDENCE, DERIVED BY EXECUTION. Digest 581758f45921b40a was produced by running
// node_criteria_digest against the live amended node on this tree, not transcribed or
// hand-computed; the discriminating control for the pin itself is that two earlier wordings of the
// same row yield different values -- d2e455ab49e472e2 before any amendment, f0f450f4ddc82fe9 before
// the review-46995 correction to the denominator clause -- so the stored digest demonstrably tracks
// the criteria text rather than merely existing, and each correction to the bar has been re-derived
// rather than carried forward. All 19 witnesses in
// test.claim.v1_interpreter_primitive_surface_witness_test executed green on a binary REBUILT AT
// THIS HEAD, which matters more here than in most acceptances: the roster derives from macro token
// lists compiled INTO the binary, so a stale target answers about a tree that no longer exists, and
// that is exactly how #7575's shortfall stayed invisible to local runs. The named red_control is
// shadow_detector_catches_a_planted_duplicate, the planted-defect RED discharging this row's second
// red_control arm, paired with shadow_detector_admits_a_real_alias_pair so that a detector refusing
// legitimate aliases would fail too.
//
// WHAT THIS RECEIPT DOES NOT CLAIM. The first red_control arm -- an arm without a row and a row
// without an arm are both unwritable -- is discharged BY CONSTRUCTION, one macro token list
// expanding both, and therefore has no executing witness by design; the node's own first_slice
// already states it is mechanically preventive for the sites it covers and explicitly not
// structural, because a whole new dispatch site can still be added in Rust unnoticed. Closing that
// is v1-interpreter-primitive-dispatch-authority, which is NOT implemented and NOT accepted here --
// and the record needs one correction, because a rollup briefly read it as landed: PR #7624 carried
// the title 'Lane B: interpreter roster R1 -- invert dispatch authority from Rust to the .dag
// roster', but its entire merged diff is two lines adding `import std.types { NonEmptyStr }` to
// this carrier. It inverted no authority, generated no dispatch, and deleted no Rust-side
// denominator; it is a harmless missing-import repair whose title described work that was never
// written (operator correction, 2026-08-02). R1 is unstarted, so accepting R0 here advances the
// frontier to an unimplemented successor rather than past it. The third arm -- a roster able to
// represent an arm carrying no semantic primitive identity -- holds STRUCTURALLY, because
// InterpreterPrimitiveDispatchArm carries an interpreter-local arm identity and no
// semantic-primitive-identity field at all, so every row already is such an arm and the closing
// contract shows the roster's operations are total over one. An earlier cut of that clause
// discharged it with D1's primitive_d0_derived_rows_missing_identity_count() > 0, which measures
// how far the semantic identity join has got rather than what the roster can carry, and would have
// gone red exactly when the join succeeded for every derived row -- legitimate downstream progress
// reddening an upstream node's acceptance check, the same defect class #7615 removed from the
// census witnesses relocated onto join state (caught in review 46974, corrected before merge). The
// seed-file reconciliation clause is delivered as a construction plus a named residue (every
// primitive arm now carries the token form arm "identity" { "spelling" } =>, so the residue is
// whatever stays bare) and is NOT asserted by an executing witness; that is a real limit of this
// handback, stated rather than implied.

type RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjection
  = RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjected { receipts: List<RoadmapAcceptanceReceipt> }
  | RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjectionRefused { detail: String }

data acceptance_history_replay_refused_detail: String = "acceptance history replay refused: an event did not apply to the replayed state, so no live receipt set can be derived from this history"

fn roadmap_acceptance_event_history_load() -> RoadmapAcceptanceEventHistoryLoad {
  load_roadmap_acceptance_event_history(path: roadmap_acceptance_event_history_carrier_repo_path)
}

// roadmap_acceptance_event_history_load is the single authority for acceptance facts, loading from
// dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_acceptance_event_history.jsonl (append-only JSONL carrier).
// roadmap_acceptance_receipts projects the live receipt set by replaying recorded and revoked
// events only — never a second editable receipt list; LoadRefused is a typed
// RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjectionRefused, never an empty receipt list. Withheld node ids
// from the same carrier refuse the same way: LoadRefused is WithheldNodeIdsProjectionRefused,
// never [] (empty withheld would widen the active frontier). New acceptance appends
// AcceptanceRecorded to the carrier; withdrawal appends AcceptanceRevoked naming the exact prior
// receipt, a disposition, and audit fields. Authored seal count/digest are deleted: prefix law is
// enforced by gunbc.roadmap_acceptance_history_observation parsing merge-base carrier content via
// git.Core.Show — a changed carrier without valid merge-base parse refuses
// AcceptanceHistoryIntegrityRefusedPriorHistoryObservation, never prior_history = [].

// The single route from a loaded event history to a receipts projection. Both wrappers below
// call it, so a loaded-but-unreplayable history cannot fabricate an affirmative empty on one
// path while the other refuses: there is one arm to get wrong, not two.
fn acceptance_receipts_projection_from_events(
  events: List<RoadmapAcceptanceEvent>,
) -> RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjection {
  match project_live_acceptance_receipts(history: events) {
    LiveAcceptanceReceiptsReplayed { live: live } =>
      RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjected { receipts: live }
    LiveAcceptanceReceiptsReplayRefused =>
      RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjectionRefused {
        detail: acceptance_history_replay_refused_detail,
      }
  }
}


fn roadmap_acceptance_receipts(history: RoadmapAcceptanceEventHistoryLoad) -> RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjection {
  match history {
    RoadmapAcceptanceEventHistoryLoaded { events: events } =>
      acceptance_receipts_projection_from_events(events: events)
    RoadmapAcceptanceEventHistoryLoadRefused { detail: detail } =>
      RoadmapAcceptanceReceiptsProjectionRefused { detail: detail }
  }
}

fn roadmap_acceptance_history_integrity_verdict_on_authority() -> AcceptanceHistoryIntegrityVerdict {
  match roadmap_acceptance_event_history_load() {
    RoadmapAcceptanceEventHistoryLoadRefused { detail: detail } =>
      AcceptanceHistoryIntegrityRefusedPriorHistoryObservation {
        detail: concat("current history load refused: ", detail),
      }
    RoadmapAcceptanceEventHistoryLoaded { events: current } =>
      acceptance_history_integrity_verdict_from_prior_observation(
        observation: current_roadmap_acceptance_history_prior_observation(current_history: current),
        history: current,
      )
  }
}

fn roadmap_acceptance_history_integrity_holds() -> Bool {
  match roadmap_acceptance_history_integrity_verdict_on_authority() {
    AcceptanceHistoryIntegrityHolds => true
    AcceptanceHistoryIntegrityRefusedReplay => false
    AcceptanceHistoryIntegrityRefusedVanishedWithoutRevocation { receipt: _ } => false
    AcceptanceHistoryIntegrityRefusedPriorPrefix { index: _ } => false
    AcceptanceHistoryIntegrityRefusedPriorHistoryObservation { detail: _ } => false
  }
}

// ACCEPTANCE OF LANDED WORK, the exact state closing_contract_task_naming_correction_note names
// this node as the specimen of: bound, implemented, green, awaiting the operator act. The landing
// is the Gate-A oracle (#7408, operator-merged): SelfEmitted requires an executed probe receipt, so
// an emitter-produced claim without a receipt from the run that made it is unrepresentable — the
// ticket's own red control. Evidence, executed 2026-08-02 on the tree carrying this receipt: all 27
// witness fns in the contract's entry file pass, including the bound closing validation (a restored
// binding without an executed receipt does not authorize) and its sibling refusal controls
// (hand-forged authorization, minted executed verdicts, synthesized digests, fabricated
// build-greens all red). The digest f1c76111c83a0a60 was DERIVED BY EXECUTION —
// node_criteria_digest run against the live node on this tree, not transcribed. The handback's
// three elements are on main: the relabelled roster (every frontier row producer-qualified), the
// retired single count (per-producer baselines, witness_producer_qualified_baselines_holds), and
// the refusal demonstration (the _reds suite). Acceptance is the operator's merge of the change
// adding this receipt; the receipt is authored by the roadmap steward as the vehicle, never as the
// act.

// THE NODE'S HANDBACK WAS TWO THINGS -- a check that can fail, plus the decision recorded either
// way -- and only the first half existed before this change. The decision procedure landed earlier
// and is genuinely discriminating: a reflexive claim/check refuses with
// bootstrap_self_verify_same_artifact, a planted divergence with bootstrap_self_verify_diverged,
// mismatched provenance with bootstrap_self_verify_provenance_mismatch, and a distinct
// equal-content pair with equal provenance ADMITS, so none of the three refusals is a blanket red.
// seed_honesty_decision was SeedHonestyUndecided, so the aggregate stayed red and was enrolled
// QuarantineProbeExpectRed rather than reported done over the missing half. This change records the
// operator verdict of 2026-08-02, the aggregate greens, and the quarantine enrollment is deleted in
// the same commit. Digest 1717cfe258d9afe1 was DERIVED BY EXECUTION -- node_criteria_digest run
// against the live node on this tree -- and the probe carried its own control: it reproduced
// f1c76111c83a0a60 for v2-emitter-producer-provenance, byte-matching the receipt an unrelated
// change had already stored for that node, and returned three distinct values for three nodes, so
// it is computing the criteria rather than answering with a constant.
//
// THE VERDICT IS FixedPointTrustSufficient, AND THE NAME UNDERSELLS ITS CONDITIONS. It does not say
// a fixed point is trustworthy. It says v1 deletion does not require an independently implemented
// second compiler, and requires instead an explicit generation-admission pipeline: each stage
// executes against a declared requirement revision, binds its exact inputs, outputs, producer and
// verdict, and prevents downstream use when its local requirements fail; a candidate generation may
// be produced and inspected but cannot promote itself; promotion requires declared requirement
// changes, complete stage and behavioral receipts, no undeclared v1 fallback, and a retained
// known-good recovery path; breaking requirement changes require an expand-migrate-contract bridge;
// and the previous generation stays available until the promoted compiler demonstrates it can
// produce a valid CHANGED successor rather than merely reproduce itself. That program is designed
// in docs/plans/emission-admission-stage-aware-pipeline-design.md (gunbc#7681, in review at the
// time of writing, itself carrying operator-requested changes) and is UNBUILT; no roadmap node
// carries it yet. This receipt accepts the decision, not the pipeline the decision requires.
//
// THREE THINGS THE VERDICT EXPLICITLY DOES NOT PROTECT, carried on the rationale itself so a later
// reader cannot infer them from the node being green. A fixed point does not defeat a
// self-reproducing defect -- stage1 equalling stage2 is exactly what such a defect also produces,
// and that residual is ACCEPTED here rather than ruled out. seed_self_verify_check is a proven
// decision procedure and NOT the live trust-path authority: the realized-comparison and regen paths
// do not route their artifact identities through it, this change does not wire that path, and the
// generation-admission program owns it. Rollback and fix-forward are different guarantees --
// committed emitted Rust gives rollback to any earlier generation but does not by itself give
// fix-forward once a requirement revision has changed what the previous generation can read, and
// the bootstrap reachability invariant that addresses this is unbuilt today. An earlier draft of
// the rationale argued DDC was unnecessary because the seed's source is readable. That argument is
// WRONG and was corrected before this receipt: the classic attack is a binary miscompiling readable
// source, so readability is not the defense. What the rationale now claims is narrower and is a
// mitigation rather than a proof -- the emitted Rust is committed as plain text and re-compared by
// the regen gate, so a perpetuated defect must appear in a readable diff AND survive review of it.
//
// THE RED CONTROL IS A FLIPPED EXPECT-RED PROBE, NOT A NEW TEST.
// seed_honesty_undecided_keeps_closing_contract_red read the live decision constant, so the verdict
// landing left it able to assert only a state that no longer exists -- it had to go red or be
// deleted, and DESIGN 4b(4) forbids deleting the evidence with the machinery. The conjunction is
// now a function of the decision applied to the live constant by the contract and to
// SeedHonestyUndecided by the permanent control. That control is what keeps the fifth conjunct
// load-bearing: weaken the decision arm so an undecided value satisfies the contract and the
// contract stays green while only the control reds.

type RoadmapAuthorityProjection
  = RoadmapAuthorityProjected {
      doc: RoadmapDocument,
      accepted: List<RoadmapNodeId>,
      withheld: List<RoadmapNodeId>,
    }
  | RoadmapAuthorityProjectionRefused { detail: String }

// One load of the carrier produces both frontier halves. Callers that need accepted AND withheld
// consume this, never a pair of independent live projections. FrontierSetsProjected is admitted
// only after replay succeeds and every live receipt joins the current declaration authority.
type FrontierSetsProjection
  = FrontierSetsProjected { accepted: List<RoadmapNodeId>, withheld: List<RoadmapNodeId> }
  | FrontierSetsProjectionRefused { detail: String }



type WithheldNodeIdsProjection
  = WithheldNodeIdsProjected { ids: List<RoadmapNodeId> }
  | WithheldNodeIdsProjectionRefused { detail: String }



fn roadmap_node_id_set(ids: List<RoadmapNodeId>) -> Set<RoadmapNodeId> {
  ids |> fold(init: empty_set(), f: (acc, id) => set_insert(acc, id))
}

fn active_roadmap_nodes_for_accepted(
  accepted: List<RoadmapNodeId>,
  withheld: List<RoadmapNodeId>,
) -> List<RoadmapNode> {
  let accepted_set = roadmap_node_id_set(ids: accepted)
  let withheld_set = roadmap_node_id_set(ids: withheld)
  declared_roadmap_nodes() |> filter(n =>
    !set_contains(accepted_set, n.node) && !set_contains(withheld_set, n.node)
  )
}

type ActiveRoadmapNodesProjection
  = ActiveRoadmapNodesProjected { nodes: List<RoadmapNode> }
  | ActiveRoadmapNodesProjectionRefused { detail: String }

type ActiveRoadmapEdgesProjection
  = ActiveRoadmapEdgesProjected { edges: List<RoadmapEdge> }
  | ActiveRoadmapEdgesProjectionRefused { detail: String }

fn active_roadmap_nodes_projection() -> ActiveRoadmapNodesProjection {
  match frontier_sets_projection(history: roadmap_acceptance_event_history_load()) {
    FrontierSetsProjectionRefused { detail: detail } =>
      ActiveRoadmapNodesProjectionRefused { detail: detail }
    FrontierSetsProjected { accepted: accepted, withheld: withheld } =>
      ActiveRoadmapNodesProjected {
        nodes: active_roadmap_nodes_for_accepted(accepted: accepted, withheld: withheld),
      }
  }
}

fn active_roadmap_edges_projection() -> ActiveRoadmapEdgesProjection {
  match frontier_sets_projection(history: roadmap_acceptance_event_history_load()) {
    FrontierSetsProjectionRefused { detail: detail } =>
      ActiveRoadmapEdgesProjectionRefused { detail: detail }
    FrontierSetsProjected { accepted: accepted, withheld: withheld } =>
      ActiveRoadmapEdgesProjected {
        edges: active_roadmap_edges_for_accepted(accepted: accepted, withheld: withheld),
      }
  }
}

// The derived tasks are added at the PROJECTION, not to active_roadmap_nodes_for_accepted. Folding
// them into the active set reded witness_active_set_is_declared_minus_accepted and
// witness_accepted_parent_discharges_active_dependency, and those witnesses are right: 'active =
// declared minus accepted' is a law about the AUTHORITY, and a derived projection row is not a
// declared deliverable. Keeping the law intact is also what makes the derivation safe — the
// authority stays exactly what someone authored, and the frontier view is a function of it.

// THESE ARE CLOSING-CONTRACT TASKS, NOT ACCEPTANCE TASKS, and the earlier name caused a real
// category error. The projection computes active AND ExecutionContractUnspecified AND
// every-prerequisite-accepted, which is the set of nodes whose completion nothing can yet CHECK.
// Authoring that check is contract work. Acceptance is a separate, later, operator act on work
// already done and verified.
//
// The old name made those two look like one state, and a node can be in any of several: blocked on
// unaccepted prerequisites; missing a closing contract; dispatchable; implemented but
// verification-red; implemented and green, awaiting operator acceptance; accepted.
// v2-emitter-producer-provenance is the specimen that exposed it — it is BOUND, so it never appears
// in this projection, while what it actually needs is acceptance of landed work. A projection named
// for acceptance that structurally cannot surface the thing needing acceptance is misnamed, so it
// was renamed to startable_nodes_missing_closing_contract_for_accepted.
//
// THAT GAP IS CLOSED, ALONG THE LINE THIS NOTE PRESCRIBED. It read: a derived task is itself built
// with ExecutionContractUnspecified, and dispatch refuses an unspecified contract, so the belt
// could not dispatch the very rows whose job is to supply a contract — the target waited on a
// contract and the contract-authoring row had none of its own. It asked for ONE parameterized
// ClosingContractAuthoring kind reusable across every target rather than ten bespoke
// meta-witnesses, and that is what landed: gunbc.roadmap_execution_contract
// ClosingContractAuthoringValidation carries the target id and derives its argv, and
// closing_contract_task_for below binds it, so a derived task appears already dispatchable and no
// roster of per-target entry points is maintained anywhere.
//
// WHAT THE PREDICATE DECIDES IS NARROWER THAN THE WISH LIST ABOVE, AND THE BOUNDARY IS DECLARED
// RATHER THAN IMPLIED BY A GREEN: gunbc.roadmap_closing_contract_authoring
// closing_contract_authoring_predicate_note owns it. Two of the four clauses are answered as asked
// (a contract exists carrying at least one validation; every validation it names resolves). One is
// refused as undecidable by inspection and carries its next-rung trigger there — a planted failure
// showing the validation can reject is an experiment against a mutated subject, not a predicate
// over the model — and it remains the handback duty the generated ticket's own red_control states.
// One is unwritable rather than unchecked: a contract is a field ON the target node, so no
// representation lets it name a different identity, and a check for it would be the
// permanently-green decoration DESIGN 4b forbids. A fifth arm the wish list did not anticipate is
// checked because this derivation makes it reachable — a target closed by another
// closing-contract-authoring validation closes nothing about its own work, and refuses.

// CLOSING-CONTRACT TASKS ARE DERIVED, NOT AUTHORED (operator ruling 2026-07-29: 'you shouldn't need
// to manually maintain that stuff — the relationship should be inferred from one side or the
// other'). The first cut hand-wrote four acceptance nodes and four edges, and one had to be
// hand-removed within minutes because pderive-typesafe-nullary-reflection was already accepted: a
// new prerequisite on completed work retroactively un-satisfies it, which
// witness_accepted_parents_precede_dependents caught. That is the hand-maintained-count failure
// this repository refuses everywhere else — two things that must be kept in step, drifting the
// moment either side moves.
//
// The relationship is a FUNCTION of the node: startable (every prerequisite accepted) AND unbound
// (ExecutionContractUnspecified) means its closing validation does not exist, so authoring that
// validation is the work that unblocks it. Nothing else needs saying. An accepted node leaves the
// active set and its task disappears; a node that gains a contract stops being unbound and its task
// disappears; a node whose prerequisite lands becomes startable and its task appears. No cleanup
// step exists because there is nothing to clean.
//
// The frontier is deliberately READY-BUT-FOR-THE-CONTRACT rather than every unbound node. A terminus
// like v1-zero-hand-maintained-rust is unbound too, but surfacing a closing-contract task for work
// whose prerequisites are years out, or that nobody has centered, is noise that would bury the rows
// that matter today.
//
// On identity: a derived row takes a deterministic id from its target rather than a minted one, and
// that is consistent with the identity law rather than an exception to it. The law governs declared
// DELIVERABLES, which must survive rename and reordering. A closing-contract task is a projection
// of a frontier state — it has no history to preserve, it exists exactly while its target lacks a
// contract, and if the target's slug changes it is honestly a different task.

fn ticket_headline_of(n: RoadmapNode) -> String {
  match line_ticket(item: n.line) {
    Present { value: t } => t.headline
    Absent => n.node as String
  }
}

fn ticket_red_control_of(n: RoadmapNode) -> String {
  match line_ticket(item: n.line) {
    Present { value: t } => t.red_control
    Absent => "the node states no red control"
  }
}

// DO NOT 'FIX' 2-scm-compatibility-shape's rnfx_ IDENTITY PREFIX. It reads as a test-fixture prefix
// where every other declared node uses rn_, and that asymmetry is real but it is COSMETIC: no
// witness asserts any prefix, and roadmap_node_identity_law constrains what may not be an identity
// INPUT (slug, title, criteria digest, containment position, dependency neighbourhood, declaration
// order, file position), never the alphabet of the minted value.
//
// Rewriting it would be the actual violation. The law's whole persistence argument is that an
// identity must survive edits, reordering, regeneration and independent branches; mutating a live
// identity for tidiness is precisely the churn it exists to forbid, and it would silently break
// anything holding the old value. The prefix carries no meaning to any mechanism, so the honest
// disposition is to leave it and record why — which is what this row is for. A prefix convention
// worth enforcing would be enforced at MINT time by construction, not by editing identities already
// in the graph.

// A CLOSING-CONTRACT TASK IS MINTED ONLY FOR A NODE WHOSE CONTRACT IS ITS ONLY MISSING FACT
// (operator ruling, 2026-09-30). The node must be sized, rooted, centered, open and startable; the
// one thing it lacks is the check that closes it. The decision is gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_readiness
// readiness_only_contract_missing over the same node_readiness the dispatcher and the page read, so
// a container that supplies no centering, an unsized row and an unrooted one mint nothing -- the
// mechanism, not a deletion list, is what keeps a project from growing a closing-contract task.
// Dependencies here are the declared edges against the accepted set: the derived edge from a node
// to its own closing-contract task is a CONSEQUENCE of minting and cannot be its precondition.
fn unmet_parents_in(n: RoadmapNode, parents: Map<String, List<RoadmapNodeId>>, accepted: Set<RoadmapNodeId>) -> List<String> {
  match roadmap_parents_of(parents: parents, id: n.node as String) {
    Absent => []
    Present { value: ps } => ps |> filter(p => !set_contains(accepted, p)) |> map(p => p as String)
  }
}

// THE MINTING RULE AS A PURE FOLD over a supplied graph, so a witness plants the nodes it asks
// about; the live frontier below applies it to the declared authority. Every per-node question --
// accepted, withheld, lineage, unmet parents -- reads an index built once from the supplied graph.
fn closing_contract_candidates(
  nodes: List<RoadmapNode>,
  edges: List<RoadmapEdge>,
  accepted: List<RoadmapNodeId>,
  withheld: List<RoadmapNodeId>,
) -> List<RoadmapNode> {
  let accepted_set = roadmap_node_id_set(ids: accepted)
  let withheld_set = roadmap_node_id_set(ids: withheld)
  let index = lineage_index(nodes: nodes)
  let fuel = count(nodes) + 1
  let parents = roadmap_parent_index(edges: edges)
  nodes
    |> filter(n => !set_contains(accepted_set, n.node))
    |> filter(n => !set_contains(withheld_set, n.node))
    |> filter(n => readiness_may_be_only_contract_missing(node: n, merged: []))
    |> filter(n => readiness_only_contract_missing(r: node_readiness(
      node: n,
      lineage: lineage_walk_in(node_id: n.node as String, index: index, fuel: fuel),
      unmet_parents: unmet_parents_in(n: n, parents: parents, accepted: accepted_set),
      merged: [],
    )))
}

fn startable_nodes_missing_closing_contract_for_accepted(
  accepted: List<RoadmapNodeId>,
  withheld: List<RoadmapNodeId>,
) -> List<RoadmapNode> {
  closing_contract_candidates(nodes: declared_roadmap_nodes(), edges: declared_roadmap_edges(), accepted: accepted, withheld: withheld)
}

// THE DERIVED BRIEF NAMES ITS MECHANISM, because the first one dispatched did not and the worker spent
// all forty rounds finding it (srv1, 2026-09-06, attempt a0dd151e221290a9e for ci-required-run-cost:
// forty tool calls, no edit, verification false on an unchanged tree). What the acceptance predicate
// closing_contract_authored actually needs is two edits -- a witness module with the planted RED arms,
// and the gated row in THIS file bound through its execution parameter to gunbc_claim_execution_contract --
// and the brief said neither, while its "files likely touched" pointed at the parent's own carrier.
// The witness path and module name are derived from the node id here so the brief, the check and
// the worker agree on one spelling; the exemplar row named below is a real bound row to copy from.
data closing_contract_witness_dir: String = "dag/test/claim/closing"
data closing_contract_witness_module_root: String = "test.claim.closing"
data closing_contract_exemplar_bound_row: String = "shell-dag-cron-entry-line-builder"

fn closing_contract_witness_stem(node_id: String) -> String {
  concat(join(node_id.split(sep: "-"), "_"), "_closing_contract_witness_test")
}

fn closing_contract_witness_path(node_id: String) -> String {
  join([closing_contract_witness_dir, "/", closing_contract_witness_stem(node_id: node_id), ".dag"], "")
}

fn closing_contract_witness_module(node_id: String) -> String {
  join([closing_contract_witness_module_root, ".", closing_contract_witness_stem(node_id: node_id)], "")
}

fn closing_contract_first_slice(n: RoadmapNode) -> String {
  let id = n.node as String
  join([
    "Two edits, in this order, then the acceptance command. (1) Author ", closing_contract_witness_path(node_id: id),
    " with the first line `module ", closing_contract_witness_module(node_id: id),
    "`, one `test fn` per clause of the bar, each proving its refusal on a PLANTED input (a constructed fixture the clause must refuse; never a count copied from the live tree). ",
    "(2) In dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_authority.dag find the row `id: \"", id,
    "\"` and give its roadmap_node(...) call the argument `execution: gunbc_claim_execution_contract(entry: \"", closing_contract_witness_path(node_id: id),
    "\" as FilePath, function: \"<one test fn name>\" as NonEmptyStr)`; the row `id: \"", closing_contract_exemplar_bound_row,
    "\"` in the same file is a bound row to copy the shape from. Do not touch the parent's own carriers.",
  ], "")
}

fn closing_contract_task_for(n: RoadmapNode) -> RoadmapNode {
  RoadmapNode {
    identity: concat(n.identity as String, "-closing-contract") as RoadmapNodeIdentity,
    node: concat(n.node as String, "-closing-contract") as RoadmapNodeId,
    line: TicketLine {
      done: false,
      ticket: fields(
        headline: concat("Write the closing check for: ", ticket_headline_of(n: n)),
        boundary: concat("One executable check states when this is done, transcribed from the node's own bar: ", ticket_red_control_of(n: n)),
        displaced_cost: "Turns a prose bar into a runnable verdict, so the node it gates can be dispatched and its completion checked rather than judged.",
        first_slice: closing_contract_first_slice(n: n),
        red_control: concat("The check must fail when the condition fails: ", ticket_red_control_of(n: n)),
        out_of_scope: "Write the statement of done; do not do the work it gates. Do not edit any file the parent names as its own carrier.",
        handback: "Return the executable check, each arm firing on a planted input, and the gated node bound to it."
      ),
    },
    carriers: concat(
      [
        Pointer { label: "authority", path: "dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_authority.dag" },
        Pointer { label: "witness", path: closing_contract_witness_path(node_id: n.node as String) },
      ],
      map(n.carriers, c => Pointer { label: "subject", path: c.path }),
    ),
    dispatch: Dispatchable {
      repo: "gunbc",
      acceptance: ManualAcceptance,
    },
    execution: closing_contract_authoring_contract(target: n.node as NonEmptyStr),
    owner: n.owner,
    signoff: Unsigned,
    parent: Present { value: Closes { target: n.node as String } },
    centering: n.centering,
  }
}

// The family claim, stated once (presentation family-once grouping): every closing-contract task
// shares this claim, and each generated ticket's fused headline ('Write the closing check for: X')
// repeats it per row. The pair surface below exposes the DISCRIMINATING SUBJECT (the gated node's
// own typed ticket headline) beside each generated task so a projection can state the claim once
// and never recover the subject by parsing the fused headline. The generated ticket itself is
// unchanged — ROADMAP.md rendering and dispatch briefs keep the full self-contained headline.

data closing_contract_family_claim: String = "Write the closing check — one executable done-condition for each gated node below"

type ClosingContractTaskPair {
  task: RoadmapNode
  subject: String
}

type ClosingContractTaskPairsProjection
  = ClosingContractTaskPairsProjected { pairs: List<ClosingContractTaskPair> }
  | ClosingContractTaskPairsProjectionRefused { detail: String }

type ClosingContractTaskEdgesProjection
  = ClosingContractTaskEdgesProjected { edges: List<RoadmapEdge> }
  | ClosingContractTaskEdgesProjectionRefused { detail: String }

fn closing_contract_task_pairs_for_accepted(
  accepted: List<RoadmapNodeId>,
  withheld: List<RoadmapNodeId>,
) -> List<ClosingContractTaskPair> {
  startable_nodes_missing_closing_contract_for_accepted(accepted: accepted, withheld: withheld)
    |> map(n => ClosingContractTaskPair {
      task: closing_contract_task_for(n: n),
      subject: ticket_headline_of(n: n),
    })
}

fn closing_contract_task_pairs_projection() -> ClosingContractTaskPairsProjection {
  match frontier_sets_projection(history: roadmap_acceptance_event_history_load()) {
    FrontierSetsProjectionRefused { detail: detail } =>
      ClosingContractTaskPairsProjectionRefused { detail: detail }
    FrontierSetsProjected { accepted: accepted, withheld: withheld } =>
      ClosingContractTaskPairsProjected {
        pairs: closing_contract_task_pairs_for_accepted(accepted: accepted, withheld: withheld),
      }
  }
}

fn closing_contract_task_edges_projection() -> ClosingContractTaskEdgesProjection {
  match frontier_sets_projection(history: roadmap_acceptance_event_history_load()) {
    FrontierSetsProjectionRefused { detail: detail } =>
      ClosingContractTaskEdgesProjectionRefused { detail: detail }
    FrontierSetsProjected { accepted: accepted, withheld: withheld } =>
      ClosingContractTaskEdgesProjected {
        edges: closing_contract_task_edges_for_accepted(accepted: accepted, withheld: withheld),
      }
  }
}

type ProjectedRoadmapDependencyEdgesProjection
  = ProjectedRoadmapDependencyEdgesProjected { edges: List<RoadmapEdge> }
  | ProjectedRoadmapDependencyEdgesProjectionRefused { detail: String }

// Machine-consumed dependency edges for dispatch, program view, and frontier startability: declared
// active edges plus derived closing-contract task edges. Consumers must read this projection rather
// than declared_roadmap_edges alone — hand-maintained declared edges omit closing-contract
// dependencies that the authority derives from acceptance state.

fn projected_roadmap_dependency_edges_projection() -> ProjectedRoadmapDependencyEdgesProjection {
  match frontier_sets_projection(history: roadmap_acceptance_event_history_load()) {
    FrontierSetsProjectionRefused { detail: detail } =>
      ProjectedRoadmapDependencyEdgesProjectionRefused { detail: detail }
    FrontierSetsProjected { accepted: accepted, withheld: withheld } =>
      ProjectedRoadmapDependencyEdgesProjected {
        edges: projected_roadmap_edges_for_accepted(accepted: accepted, withheld: withheld),
      }
  }
}

fn closing_contract_task_edges_for_accepted(
  accepted: List<RoadmapNodeId>,
  withheld: List<RoadmapNodeId>,
) -> List<RoadmapEdge> {
  startable_nodes_missing_closing_contract_for_accepted(accepted: accepted, withheld: withheld)
    |> map(n => RoadmapEdge {
      child: n.node,
      parent: concat(n.node as String, "-closing-contract") as RoadmapNodeId,
    })
}

fn active_roadmap_edges_for_accepted(
  accepted: List<RoadmapNodeId>,
  withheld: List<RoadmapNodeId>,
) -> List<RoadmapEdge> {
  let accepted_set = roadmap_node_id_set(ids: accepted)
  let withheld_set = roadmap_node_id_set(ids: withheld)
  declared_roadmap_edges() |> filter(e =>
    !set_contains(accepted_set, e.child)
      && !set_contains(accepted_set, e.parent)
      && !set_contains(withheld_set, e.child)
      && !set_contains(withheld_set, e.parent)
  )
}

fn preamble() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    HeadingBlock { level: H1, inlines: [TextInline { text: "gunbc — Roadmap" }] },
    ParagraphBlock { inlines: [TextInline { text: "`DESIGN.md` is the authority for why. This file projects the remaining committed deliverables and their dependency order from `dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_authority.dag`. The authority declares every node and records a typed acceptance receipt per accepted node; the active set is derived as declared minus validly accepted minus withheld (revoked receipts whose disposition keeps a superseded node off the frontier), so acceptance never deletes a row and this projection stays active-only. It carries no mutable pull-request, CI, session, or attempt state. Implementation evidence does not equal acceptance; a merged but unaccepted deliverable remains active." }] },
    ParagraphBlock { inlines: [TextInline { text: "Indented rows depend on the row above them. Some deliverables have additional cross-chain prerequisites represented by `RoadmapEdge` in the authority. Every active row requires explicit manual acceptance. Automatic admission from this refreshed authority is future work; the existing manual dispatch path remains live and outside this model slice. Ready may schedule only after every dependency is accepted; it never overrides a dependency or refusal. Parked plans remain reachable through the documentation graph but are not active roadmap rows." }] },
    ParagraphBlock { inlines: [TextInline { text: "Parked context (non-dispatchable): [compiler algorithm survey](docs/plans/compiler-algorithm-survey-2026-07-04.md) · [dispatch maintain CC](docs/plans/dispatch-maintain-cc.md) · [eval bind proposal](docs/plans/eval-bind-node-eval-propose.md) · [interpreter memory chronicle](docs/plans/interpreter-memory-chronicle.md) · [node/subtree visibility grants](docs/plans/node-subtree-visibility-grants.md) · [non-fold residue catalogue](docs/plans/non-fold-irreducible-residue-catalogue.md) · [post-engine roadmap](docs/plans/post-engine-pr-roadmap.md) · [post-zero regen placement](docs/plans/post-zero-regen-gate-placement.md) · [syntactic sample audit](docs/plans/real-debt-syntactic-sample-audit.md) · [regen 31/32 reconciliation](docs/plans/regen-divergence-31-vs-32-reconciliation.md) · [roadmap spawner](docs/plans/roadmap-spawner.md) · [seed honesty discharge](docs/plans/seed-honesty-discharge-design.md) · [space lens minimal project](docs/plans/space-lens-minimal-project.md)." }] },
  ]
}

fn projected_roadmap_nodes_for_accepted(
  accepted: List<RoadmapNodeId>,
  withheld: List<RoadmapNodeId>,
) -> List<RoadmapNode> {
  concat(
    active_roadmap_nodes_for_accepted(accepted: accepted, withheld: withheld),
    startable_nodes_missing_closing_contract_for_accepted(accepted: accepted, withheld: withheld)
      |> map(n => closing_contract_task_for(n: n)),
  )
}

fn projected_roadmap_edges_for_accepted(
  accepted: List<RoadmapNodeId>,
  withheld: List<RoadmapNodeId>,
) -> List<RoadmapEdge> {
  concat(
    active_roadmap_edges_for_accepted(accepted: accepted, withheld: withheld),
    closing_contract_task_edges_for_accepted(accepted: accepted, withheld: withheld),
  )
}

fn active_section_for_acceptance(
  accepted: List<RoadmapNodeId>,
  withheld: List<RoadmapNodeId>,
) -> RoadmapSection {
  let nodes = projected_roadmap_nodes_for_accepted(accepted: accepted, withheld: withheld)
  let edges = projected_roadmap_edges_for_accepted(accepted: accepted, withheld: withheld)
  RoadmapSection {
    title: "Active deliverables — dependency order",
    elements: [
        section_prose(content: "Every node declares its parent in the functional decomposition: gunbc-project-root, then four projects -- v2-compiler-live (the self-hosted compiler goes live), fabric-bringup (our own fleet as a fabric, with the end-of-year VM service and fabric storage under it), debt-payoff (known gaps and recurring failure modes retired against their triggers) and factory (the machine that lands and verifies work). Readiness is derived, never authored: a node is ready to dispatch only when it is rooted, centered, sized, bound to a closing contract, open, and every prerequisite is closed (gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_readiness). Node fields define boundary, first slice, RED control, exclusions, owner, and handback."),
      section_group(label: "", nodes: nodes, edges: edges),
    ],
  }
}

fn accepted_node_ids_from_receipts(receipts: List<RoadmapAcceptanceReceipt>) -> List<RoadmapNodeId> {
  let index = receipts_by_node(receipts: receipts)
  declared_roadmap_nodes()
    |> filter(n => node_accepted_in(index: index, rn: n))
    |> map(n => n.node)
}


fn roadmap_authority_projection(history: RoadmapAcceptanceEventHistoryLoad) -> RoadmapAuthorityProjection {
  match frontier_sets_projection(history: history) {
    FrontierSetsProjectionRefused { detail: detail } =>
      RoadmapAuthorityProjectionRefused { detail: detail }
    FrontierSetsProjected { accepted: accepted, withheld: withheld } =>
      RoadmapAuthorityProjected {
        doc: RoadmapDocument {
          preamble: preamble(),
          lead_lanes: [],
          sections: [active_section_for_acceptance(
            accepted: accepted,
            withheld: withheld,
          )],
        },
        accepted: accepted,
        withheld: withheld,
      }
  }
}

// STATE vs WORK, and IN-GRAPH vs BESIDE-IT. Rung STATE has one authority — the guarantee claims
// carrier (ladder-claims-carrier node), its dispositions DERIVED from execution receipts and
// EMITTED by ladder-census-emitters — never a ticket; a climb node names its target transition and
// lets the census/carrier say where the class stands today (the 2026-07-31 operator verdict on
// gunbc#7489 caught headlines transcribing rungs, the third rung-inflation catch in this lane's own
// artifacts). The WORK rows below are ordinary declared roadmap nodes under lane owner
// compiler-guarantee: the first cut rendered them from a section-local list beside the graph, which
// minted a second roadmap universe — uncounted, receipt-unreachable, invisible to the
// identity/endpoint/acyclicity witnesses and to active_roadmap_nodes() (same verdict; review 45356
// independently flagged the owner-less rows the placement forced). Owner here is the LANE's
// identity, not an assignee: nodes are unbound until dispatch binds a session and a closing
// contract, exactly like every other lane. Page visibility is
// the page's derived readiness (the operator's 2026-09-30 ruling removed the focus row), and
// rendered-node containment is witnessed — see roadmap_rendered_containment_note. Post-merge
// verdict (2026-07-31, on the merged gunbc#7489): v2-phase-carriers split into five staged nodes
// (its identity is the registry's first tombstone, superseded by v2-self-grounding-frontier);
// floor-parse-formation-wall, floor-record-construction-wall, and the
// compiler-accepted-obligation-closure terminal added; baseline anchored to the merge commit; and
// the standing discipline that an OPEN PR is candidate evidence, never main's rung — tickets cite
// open work as 'open candidate', and the census states main's disposition separately. Spine verdict
// (2026-08-01, seventh pass): the ladder-measurement-schema node now precedes probes and carrier
// (the receipt protocol both meet through); the three merged P0 slices are recut at their actual
// grain with tombstoned identities (floor-call-shape-wall, floor-method-existence-wall,
// floor-inhabitance-wall each split into an ACCEPTED fragment node plus open siblings); the
// Accepted door is split mechanism/floor/extended (compiler-accepted-obligation-closure tombstoned)
// so the audit form lands with the carrier; exemption removal re-grounded on the two grounding
// nodes; emitters parallel with the baseline; the baseline a two-revision execution. Correction on
// the split (review 45918): the first extended-activation cut kept requires-all edges on one node
// while its prose promised class-by-class widening — the graph would have deferred every admission
// behind the slowest climb; recut as four per-class admission nodes (each ready the day its own
// climb lands) with accepted-extended-obligation-closure the terminal roster-completeness
// certification, where requires-all honestly belongs. Sequencing: everything outside the spine's
// first increment (schema, then probes + carrier) and the authorized parallel containment
// workstream WAITS.

